    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 4632,
      "versionNonce": 1139402442,
      "index": "bCj",
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "tDmFtgx9b0JEKpWJp5gH7",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 6260.822059369331,
      "y": -1087.9897984137715,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 1875.155029296875,
      "height": 1135.7851239669421,
      "seed": 776670723,
      "groupIds": [
        "03FlPufbLW07YxGmVublB"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1729211769708,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "fontSize": 37.85950413223141,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "text": "\nThe ModelDescr basically describes a zip structure. But it isn't a functional unit.\nThe PredictionPipeline is the one that resolves the weights and the architecture,\nand can create a model object to run inference.\n\nA fully functional object should be the one that has the metadata of the model (e.g. name, authors),\nhas a model object (nn.Module), and knows the preprocessing, and the postprocessing strategy.\nThis is the PredictionPipeline object, that has as an attribute the ModelDescr.\n\nBut, the ModelDescr combines 3 things. The metadata, validation data, and a serialized version\nof a model by pointing to files for the weights and architecture. By combining these 3 things,\nwe need to carry along the ModelDescr to be able to get a PredictionPipeline, which means\ncarrying the validation data as well.\n\nThe validation data, should be needed only as a prerequisite to get a valid functional object.\nWe don't need to carry them along. This will cost us in memory and redundant validation checks\nif file path exists.\n\nTo make our lives easier, all the files could be in the same hierarchy with the rdf.yml.\n\nA PredictionPipeline should be the source of truth and the ModelDescr is the serialized version\nof it.\n\n",
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "\nThe ModelDescr basically describes a zip structure. But it isn't a functional unit.\nThe PredictionPipeline is the one that resolves the weights and the architecture,\nand can create a model object to run inference.\n\nA fully functional object should be the one that has the metadata of the model (e.g. name, authors),\nhas a model object (nn.Module), and knows the preprocessing, and the postprocessing strategy.\nThis is the PredictionPipeline object, that has as an attribute the ModelDescr.\n\nBut, the ModelDescr combines 3 things. The metadata, validation data, and a serialized version\nof a model by pointing to files for the weights and architecture. By combining these 3 things,\nwe need to carry along the ModelDescr to be able to get a PredictionPipeline, which means\ncarrying the validation data as well.\n\nThe validation data, should be needed only as a prerequisite to get a valid functional object.\nWe don't need to carry them along. This will cost us in memory and redundant validation checks\nif file path exists.\n\nTo make our lives easier, all the files could be in the same hierarchy with the rdf.yml.\n\nA PredictionPipeline should be the source of truth and the ModelDescr is the serialized version\nof it.\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
